foreseen
========
The gemini browser that looks into the future.

Requirements
============
C++ compiler, make, openssl, ncurses

Installation
============
Edit config.mk to match your local setup if necessary for building (foreseen
will be installed into /usr/local/bin by default).

Then run the following command to build and install foreseen:
    make install

(the above command may require root depending on the installation location)

Usage
=====
foreseen [--help] [--version] [--cache-pages VAR] [--prefetch-links VAR] \
            [--search-engine VAR] destination

Positional arguments:
  destination           page to open [nargs=0..1]

Optional arguments:
  -h, --help            shows help message and exits

  -v, --version         prints version information and exits

  -c, --cache-pages     max number of pages for each cache (history, prefetch) \
                        [nargs=0..1] [default: 500]

  -p, --prefetch-links  max number of links to prefetch per page (0 disables \ 
                        prefetching) [nargs=0..1] [default: 10]

  -e, --search-engine   default search engine [nargs=0..1] \
                        [default: "gemini://tlgs.one/search"]


Caching
=======
All caching is done in memory with a configurable fifo data structure to limit
cached page count. There are two seperate instances of the cache object at any
given time. One for prefetching and one for history.

Pages fetched with an identity are never cached, and prefetching never sends
requests to hosts you'd use an identity for.

    Prefetch caching:

    One of the main reasons this browser feels so much faster than other gemini
    protocol browsers is because of its optional prefetch caching. This is
    enabled by default. When you visit a site without an identity, a thread will
    be created that fetches the first 10 links on the page, caching them  if
    they return a 2X status code. This enables ~ instantaneous visitation of
    those links. Prefetch caching is only enabled when you aren't using an
    identity for a domain. Per the gemtext specification, "clients must not 
    automatically make any network connections as part of displaying links", 
    so this mode of operation may be construed as non-compliant with the 
    gemtext specification. I don't believe a file format specification should be
    prescriptive about such things. Additionally, if this was a feature in a
    http-based web browser, I would disable it. I perceive Gemini as being 
    more trustworthy right now and thus am not concerned about having this 
    defaulted on. 

    History caching:

    When you visit a page, the page will be cached after it's loaded. 
    If the page was in the prefetch cache before, it will be upgraded to the
    history cache. This is because visited pages are more important than
    prefetched ones. History caching is disabled when using an identity.

Identities
==========
These are specified in:
    $HOME/.gmi/{hostname}/client.crt
    $HOME/.gmi/{hostname}/client.key

Or for a non-standard port (port != 1965):
    $HOME/.foreseen/{hostname}:{port}/client.crt
    $HOME/.foreseen/{hostname}:{port}/client.key

Example:
    > tree .foreseen
    .foreseen
    └── bbs.geminispace.org
        ├── client.crt
        └── client.key

Example command to generate keys:
    openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 3650 -keyout client.key \
    -out client.crt -subj "/CN=localhost"
